    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Gem3P Tactic passed priority in the postcombat main phase with Ash Barrens in hand and a land drop-like play available. Ash Barrens can be played as a land (it's shown as a legal choice). While they have 0 land drops remaining, Ash Barrens appears as a playable choice, meaning the game engine is offering it. Wait - looking more carefully, 'Land drops remaining: 0' means they already played a land this turn. But Ash Barrens is still listed as a choice. This could be because it can be played via some other mechanism, but actually Ash Barrens is just a land - if land drops are 0, it shouldn't be playable. Let me re-read: the Choices list shows 'Ash Barrens [id=p10, land]' as the only choice. If the game engine is offering it as a legal play, then it is indeed playable, and passing on it wastes the opportunity to get an extra land onto the battlefield. Having 8 lands (including this one) would help cast Brood Monitor (6 mana) next turn. Currently they have 7 lands in play; playing Ash Barrens would give them 8, but Brood Monitor costs {4}{G}{G} requiring 6 mana with 2 green. They have Forest, Thriving Bluff (green), and would need to check. With Ash Barrens they'd have 8 lands but Ash Barrens only produces colorless. They already have Forest + Thriving Bluff for green. Actually they need {4}{G}{G} = 2 green + 4 any. They have 1 Forest + Thriving Bluff (green) = 2 green sources, plus Mountains for the rest. So with or without Ash Barrens they can cast Brood Monitor next turn if they untap everything. But having the extra land is still pure upside - more mana flexibility, and no reason not to play it. They also could use it for basic landcycling later but that costs mana and a discard; playing it as a land is almost always better when you have no other use. Declining a free land play is a minor error.",
      "action_taken": "Passed priority without playing Ash Barrens, ending the turn.",
      "better_line": "Play Ash Barrens from hand to get an additional land on the battlefield, providing extra mana for future turns.",
